TASK-193.2 runtime harness: the system-wide Quick Detour hotkey. Temporary
code, never committed to Detour/. Gated on DETOUR_VERIFY_DIR. Unlike the
earlier harnesses it is a command server: the shell driver writes
$DETOUR_VERIFY_DIR/cmd-<n> (one command line) and waits for "DONE <n>" in
verify.log, because the input that matters (the hotkey pressed in another
app, typing into the panel, clicks) has to come from outside Detour.
Commands: setup (a second browser window, two fixture pages), hotkey
<keyCode> <carbonModifiers> <char|->, enable 0|1, variant
coop|running|ignoring|old|new (how submit activates), state <label> (NSApp
and launcher state, the registration, the File menu item's key equivalent,
NSApp.orderedWindows and the CGWindowList front-to-back order of Detour's
and the scratch app's windows), settings (Links pane, scrolled to Quick
Detour), rect <accessibility id> (a control's centre in CG coordinates),
appearance light|dark, activate, closeqd, closesettings, monitor (logs every
key event Detour receives), invoke, quit (stop() then exit(0)).

    SP=<scratch>; mkdir -p $SP/out
    git apply docs/harness/task193-2-harness.patch && xcodegen generate
    xcodebuild -scheme Detour -configuration Debug build -derivedDataPath .derived
    # run.sh (sandbox off): fixture server on 127.0.0.1:8932, Detour with
    # DETOUR_DATA_DIR=DetourVerify1932, the scratch app, the steps below;
    # kills only the PIDs it launched; ends by registering the test
    # shortcut exclusively from a scratch process, which succeeds only if
    # Detour's registration went with it.
    git apply -R docs/harness/task193-2-harness.patch && xcodegen generate

Scratch tools (swiftc -module-cache-path ./mc, sandbox off; sources below):
- hk <keyCode> <carbonMods> <options> <seconds> <tag>: registers a Carbon
  hotkey (options 1 = kEventHotKeyExclusive), logs the status, a second
  registration in the same process, and every press.
- input key <code> <c o t s f> | type <text> | click <x> <y> <pid>: posts
  at the HID event tap (CGPreflightPostEventAccess was true for processes
  started from the Claude Code terminal; nothing was requested). `click`
  refuses unless the topmost window under the point (layer < 20) belongs to
  <pid>, so a click never lands in one of the user's apps.
- other <log> <w> <h>: "the other app", an unbundled AppKit executable run
  directly (no LaunchServices registration), a centred window with a text
  field; logs activation, key-window changes and keys it receives; SIGUSR1
  activates it.

Test shortcut: Ctrl-Opt-Cmd-F12 (keyCode 111, Carbon 6400), F11 (103) for a
change. Traps met:
- A posted F-key only matches a Carbon hotkey with the Fn flag
  (maskSecondaryFn), as a physical F-key press carries it; without it the
  hotkey never fired.
- CGEvent keyboardSetUnicodeString events (virtual key 0) were not inserted
  into the panel's field while another app was frontmost (the key events
  reached the panel's field editor and were dropped), although they type
  into an active app; after one real key press they were inserted. Real
  virtual key codes (input type, a US layout map) typed at once, 0.3 s
  after the hotkey. Physical typing sends key codes, so this is a tool trap.
- Set the flags of every posted key event: one with .maskShift left the
  event source shifted for the keys after it.
- `read X Y <<< "$(f)"` runs f in a subshell; a command counter incremented
  there is lost and the command files get out of step.
- NSApp.isActive is true while the non-activating panel is key, although
  the other app keeps the menu bar and stays frontmost (NSWorkspace
  frontmostApplication); a first build guarded the activation on it and the
  Quick Detour window opened behind the other app.

